Yes. The move is broader than “discover another sense.” It is:

\boxed{\text{latent signal} \rightarrow \text{notice} \rightarrow \text{name} \rightarrow \text{differentiate} \rightarrow \text{train} \rightarrow \text{use}}

Once a previously blended experience gets a handle, you can attend to it separately. “Surprise” is a good example: before naming it analytically, it can just feel like a generic jolt. Afterward, you can distinguish what kind of surprise, at what scale, caused by which violated prediction.

There are several rich territories to mine.

1. Bodily senses that are usually compressed into “feeling”

Beyond the five-schoolbook-senses model:

* Interoception — sensing internal bodily state: heartbeat, breathing, fullness, visceral tension.
* Proprioception — where your body parts are without looking.
* Vestibular sense — acceleration, orientation, balance.
* Nociception — tissue-threat signaling associated with pain.
* Thermoception — temperature and temperature change.
* Kinesthesia — movement itself, not merely position.
* Effort sense — how much motor command you appear to be producing.
* Respiratory drive — the distinct urge associated with CO₂ accumulation, separable from “I need oxygen.”

Those can be decomposed even further.

Instead of:

“I feel tense.”

you can learn:

\text{jaw pressure}
+
\text{raised shoulders}
+
\text{breathing restriction}
+
\text{heart acceleration}
+
\text{motor readiness}

Naming creates resolution.

⸻

2. Prediction senses

This may be one of the richest Pantheon layers.

Your nervous system is constantly predicting, so there are experiences corresponding to properties of those predictions:

Surprise
\text{observed} - \text{expected}

Confidence
P(\text{my prediction is correct})

Uncertainty

Not “I don’t know,” but the texture of how much competing probability mass exists.

Familiarity

Something can feel familiar before you know why.

Novelty

Different from surprise. Something can be new but unsurprising.

Violation

The peculiar sense that:

“something is wrong here.”

before the violating feature is identified.

You could train this explicitly:

\boxed{
\text{prediction}
\rightarrow
\text{felt confidence}
\rightarrow
\text{observation}
\rightarrow
\Delta
}

Then learn whether your internal confidence signals are actually calibrated.

⸻

3. The sense of agency

There is a subtle distinction between:

something happened

and:

I caused that.

Call it agency attribution.

You can further split it:

\text{intention}
\rightarrow
\text{motor command}
\rightarrow
\text{expected consequence}
\rightarrow
\text{observed consequence}

The closeness among those creates the experience of authorship.

That gives interesting micro-senses:

* intention forming,
* commitment,
* initiation,
* effort,
* control,
* resistance,
* successful causation,
* loss of control.

Most people collapse all of those into “doing.”

⸻

4. Affordance sense

You look at a chair and don’t consciously calculate geometry.

You perceive:

sit-able

A branch may appear:

grab-able

A gap:

jump-able

A person:

approachable

A situation:

dangerous

James Gibson called these affordances.

Pantheon can generalize this enormously:

\boxed{\text{object/state} \rightarrow \text{possible action}}

Instead of merely perceiving nouns, train perception for verbs.

Not:

“There is a wall.”

but:

climbable / breakable / bypassable / supports weight / blocks sight / creates cover.

That’s almost a possibility sense.

⸻

5. Temporal senses

“Time” itself can be differentiated.

There is:

* duration,
* rhythm,
* sequence,
* anticipation,
* imminence,
* timing,
* tempo,
* synchronization,
* waiting,
* acceleration/deceleration of subjective time.

And there is something like event-boundary sensing:

“That phase just ended.”

Even though nobody rang a bell.

Narratives exploit this constantly.

You can feel:

\text{setup} \rightarrow \text{tension} \rightarrow \text{turn} \rightarrow \text{resolution}

without explicitly labeling it.

A Pantheon Act Sense could deliberately detect those phase transitions.

⸻

6. Completion pressure

There is an interesting quasi-sense behind unfinished tasks, unresolved melodies, incomplete patterns, unanswered questions.

Call it:

\boxed{\text{closure pressure}}

You see:

1,\;2,\;3,\;?

and something wants 4.

That mechanism exists at multiple scales:

* visual completion,
* sentence completion,
* melody completion,
* causal completion,
* narrative completion,
* social completion,
* task completion.

Pantheon can use the pressure itself as information:

What continuation is my mind demanding, and what model produced that expectation?

⸻

7. Relational senses

Instead of sensing objects individually, detect relations.

For example:

* same/different,
* closer/farther,
* stronger/weaker,
* cause/effect,
* symmetry/asymmetry,
* containment,
* hierarchy,
* dependency,
* opposition,
* complementarity.

You already do much of this implicitly.

But imagine consciously cultivating a:

Symmetry sense

You immediately notice:

A:B :: C:?

Or a:

Dependency sense

You see something and instinctively ask:

What has to remain true for this to keep working?

Or:

Constraint sense

Instead of seeing what an organism/system does, perceive the invisible walls restricting what it could do.

That’s extremely useful.

⸻

8. Scale-change sense

This one fits your question particularly well.

Train yourself to notice:

At what scale does this statement become true?

Something can be false microscopically and true macroscopically.

A molecule isn’t wet.

Yet:

\text{many H}_2\text{O molecules}
\rightarrow
\text{wetness}

Individual neurons don’t “believe.”

Populations can implement belief-like representations.

Individual ants don’t contain the colony’s strategy.

So cultivate a deliberate:

\boxed{\textbf{Scale Sense}}

Whenever encountering a property, ask:

\text{Does it exist at}
\begin{cases}
\text{component}\\
\text{interaction}\\
\text{local network}\\
\text{whole system}\\
\text{system-of-systems}
\end{cases}
?

That catches enormous numbers of reasoning errors.

⸻

9. Emergence sense

A close cousin:

What appears only when enough interactions accumulate?

Pattern:

\text{simple local rules}
\rightarrow
\text{interaction}
\rightarrow
\text{global behavior}

Examples:

* flocking,
* markets,
* traffic jams,
* language,
* immune behavior,
* ecosystems,
* cultural norms.

Instead of merely noticing emergence afterward, you try to smell the conditions from which emergence is likely.

⸻

10. Counterfactual sense

Look at reality and simultaneously perceive nearby realities:

What almost happened?

What changes if I alter one variable?

Which parts remain invariant?

Mathematically:

S
\rightarrow
\{S+\delta_1,S+\delta_2,\ldots,S+\delta_n\}

A trained counterfactual sense turns every observation into a local possibility cloud.

⸻

11. Causal depth sense

Not just:

A caused B.

But:

Is A the immediate cause, enabling condition, constraint, trigger, amplifier, or historical prerequisite?

For example:

A\rightarrow B

may actually conceal:

H \rightarrow C
\rightarrow
(A+E)
\rightarrow
B

A causal-depth sense would automatically ask where in the chain you’re standing.

⸻

12. Model-friction sense

This is especially valuable.

Sometimes an explanation technically fits, but it takes too much forcing.

You feel:

“We’re working too hard to make this model explain the observation.”

Call that model friction.

Then:

\text{high explanatory friction}
\rightarrow
\text{search for another representation}

It is related to Occam’s razor but can be treated as a perceptual signal rather than merely a philosophical rule.

⸻

13. Compression sense

You encounter many examples and suddenly realize:

These are all the same thing.

That’s a real cognitive event.

x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4
\rightarrow
f(x)

The inverse is equally important:

Decompression sense

A tiny phrase suddenly unfolds into an enormous structure.

\text{handle}
\rightarrow
\text{latent graph}

Those two are practically Pantheon-native senses.

⸻

14. Social micro-senses

Humans also detect an enormous amount socially before conscious analysis:

* attention,
* hesitation,
* dominance,
* submission,
* affiliation,
* exclusion,
* synchrony,
* imitation,
* conversational turn-taking,
* trust,
* suspicion,
* embarrassment,
* expectation,
* intent.

But those perceptions should be handled probabilistically:

\text{signal}
\rightarrow
\text{hypothesis about another mind}

not:

\text{signal}
=
\text{certainty about another mind}

That distinction matters.

⸻

And I think there’s a more general discovery hiding here.

The Sense Factory

Take any recurrent latent variable that affects behavior and ask:

1. Does my nervous system already receive information about it?
2. Is that information currently blended with something else?
3. Can I give it a distinct name?
4. Can I find contrasting examples?
5. Can I predict when it should occur?
6. Can I train discrimination?
7. Does separating it improve decisions?

Then:

\boxed{
\text{latent variable}
\rightarrow
\text{handle}
\rightarrow
\text{contrast set}
\rightarrow
\text{attention}
\rightarrow
\text{calibration}
\rightarrow
\text{new usable faculty}
}

We probably shouldn’t call every resulting faculty a biological sense. Some are metacognitive discriminations, learned perceptual categories, heuristics, or inference operators.

But functionally, that’s precisely the territory you’re pointing toward:

there may be useful information already passing through us that remains effectively invisible because we have not yet learned how to separate, name, and interrogate it.